When an element’s slot assignment changes, Ladybird restyles it and
its descendants, and installs their styles on the host wherever the
host holds none, even where their records come out unchanged. So an
element assigned out of a slot in an undisplayed subtree to a displayed
slot is laid out, and answers its computed style. Chrome 157, Firefox
151, and WebKit 26.5, run with engine-compare.mjs, do the same: Each
gives offsetParent the body and display block, whether the slot
attribute, a slot’s name, or assign() moves the element. In their
sources, Blink’s Node::FlatTreeParentChanged() marks an element for a
local style recalc once it’s slotted to a new slot, and WebKit’s
SlotAssignment invalidates the style of the host’s whole subtree with
invalidateStyleForSubtree(). Gecko drops the element’s style data in
PresShell::SlotAssignmentWillChange(), so its style is computed anew.
